window.NOVELS = [
 {id:'the-city-above-the-clouds',title:'The City Above the Clouds',author:'NovelNest Originals',genre:'Fantasy',tags:['Fantasy','Adventure'],status:'Completed',cover:'assets/clouds.webp',updated:'2026-10-05',sample:true,license:{type:'Original demonstration content',note:'Created for the NovelNest website demonstration.'},synopsis:'Every night, a city appears above the clouds. When a forgotten letter reaches a young mapmaker, she discovers that the impossible city has been waiting for her to find the way home.',chapters:[
 {title:'A letter from the sky',paragraphs:[
 'The letter arrived without a messenger. It lay on Mira’s windowsill at dawn, weighted down by a stone that floated an inch above the paper.',
 'She had spent seven years drawing maps of the valley. Every river, road, and orchard had a place on her desk. Yet the address on the envelope belonged to a city she had never put on any map: Aster, above the northern clouds.',
 'Inside was one sentence in her mother’s handwriting. Bring the map you have not finished.',
 'Her mother had vanished when Mira was ten. The village had called it an accident in the mountains. Mira had learned to say the same thing, because people grew uncomfortable when she asked about the lights in the sky.',
 'She opened the bottom drawer. Beneath folded bills lay the last map they had made together. Most of it was blank. In one corner, her mother had drawn a small door.',
 'As Mira held the floating stone against the paper, a path of silver ink unfolded across the empty page. It led north, beyond the edge of every road she knew.',
 'She packed bread, a compass, and two pencils. Then she locked the workshop and set out before she could talk herself into staying.' ]},
 {title:'The bridge with no stones',paragraphs:[
 'At the mountain’s summit, the silver path ended at a cliff. Beyond it stretched an ocean of cloud, pink with the last light of afternoon.',
 'Mira checked her compass. Its needle pointed upward.',
 '“That thing will not help you here,” said a voice. An old man sat beside the cliff, mending a coat with golden thread. “The bridge only carries people who know where they are going.”',
 '“I am going to Aster.”',
 '“That is a name. I asked where.”',
 'Mira looked down at the little door on her map. She remembered her mother waiting in the workshop, the smell of ink, the warm circle of lamplight. “I am going to find someone,” she said.',
 'The old man smiled. A single stone appeared beyond the cliff. Mira placed one foot on it. Another stone formed ahead, then another. She did not look down.',
 'Halfway across, the clouds parted. Towers rose from an island of pale rock, their windows bright against the evening. At the far gate, a woman held a lamp above her head.',
 'Even from that distance, Mira knew the way her mother stood when she was trying not to cry.' ]},
 {title:'A place on the map',paragraphs:[
 'For a long moment, neither of them spoke. Mira had carried so many questions up the mountain that she could not decide which one to ask first.',
 'Her mother answered the smallest one. “Yes,” she whispered. “I tried to come home.”',
 'Aster had lost its road to the valley. Its maps were beautiful and precise, but none of its mapmakers remembered what waited below. Her mother had spent seven years searching for someone who could draw both places as a single world.',
 '“You could have sent the letter sooner,” Mira said.',
 '“I should have.” There was no excuse in her voice. That made the answer easier to believe.',
 'They worked until morning. Mira drew the orchard, the river, and the workshop. Her mother drew the towers and the bridge. Where the two maps met, they left a small open door.',
 'At sunrise, a road appeared along the mountain. It was ordinary stone, wide enough for a cart and solid enough for anyone to cross.',
 'Mira took her mother’s hand. There would be time for every difficult question. First, there was a workshop to open, and a village that needed a new map.' ]}]},
 {id:'when-the-peonies-bloom',title:'When the Peonies Bloom',author:'NovelNest Originals',genre:'Romance',tags:['Romance','Historical'],status:'Completed',cover:'assets/peonies.webp',updated:'2026-10-04',sample:true,license:{type:'Original demonstration content',note:'Created for the NovelNest website demonstration.'},synopsis:'A palace gardener and a reluctant royal musician leave notes in a forgotten courtyard. Between unfinished songs and blooming peonies, an unexpected friendship begins to change their futures.',chapters:[
 {title:'The quiet courtyard',paragraphs:[
 'Every morning, Lian swept the petals from the eastern courtyard. Every evening, someone left a sheet of music on her bench.',
 'At first she thought the wind was responsible. Then she noticed the stones placed carefully at each corner, and the sentence written beneath the notes: Does this sound like spring?',
 'Lian knew nothing about music. She wrote her answer anyway. Spring is not always gentle. Sometimes it breaks things open.',
 'The next day, the melody drifting through the palace windows was different. It rose suddenly, bright and fearless, then settled into a quiet warmth.',
 'A young man stood at the far end of the garden. He carried a wooden flute and wore an expression of nervous hope.',
 '“Was that closer?” he asked.',
 'Lian leaned on her broom. “Perhaps. But you have never watched a seed grow, have you?”',
 '“No.”',
 'She handed him a small clay pot. “Then we should start there.”' ]},
 {title:'Something worth waiting for',paragraphs:[
 'His name was Ren. He had been invited to play at the royal spring ceremony, and he had not slept properly in weeks.',
 '“They expect a masterpiece,” he told her, staring at the pot.',
 '“The seed expects water,” Lian replied. “Let us solve the easier problem first.”',
 'He began visiting before his rehearsals. Some mornings they spoke. On others, they sat quietly while the palace woke around them. He learned which flowers liked the shade. She learned that a pause could be part of a song.',
 'One afternoon, an official came to inspect the courtyard. The eastern garden was to be cleared for a new hall. Lian stood still until the man left, then carefully set down her tools.',
 'Ren wanted to promise that he could stop it. Instead, he asked what she needed.',
 '“Help carrying the pots,” she said.',
 'They worked until dusk. When the final plant was safe, Ren placed the tiny seedling on the bench between them. Two green leaves had finally opened.',
 '“Something worth waiting for,” he said. Lian looked at him, and found she could smile.' ]},
 {title:'A song with room to grow',paragraphs:[
 'At the spring ceremony, Ren played a song that began with silence. The court shifted impatiently. Then a single note rose, small and steady as a green shoot.',
 'Lian listened from the doorway. She heard rain, falling petals, and footsteps approaching an empty bench. Most of all, she heard the space he had left for an answer.',
 'Afterward, the queen asked where the melody had come from. Ren told her about the eastern garden. He spoke plainly, without pleading. A week later, the new hall was moved to another part of the grounds.',
 'On the first morning of summer, Lian found no sheet of music on her bench. Ren was sitting there instead, holding two cups of tea.',
 '“I have been offered another season here,” he said. “I thought I might stay.”',
 '“For the music?”',
 '“For the gardener.”',
 'She took the cup from his hand. Beyond the courtyard wall, the palace bells began to ring. Neither of them hurried to answer. The peonies were blooming, and there was still so much to learn about growing things.' ]}]},
 {id:'the-thirteenth-hour',title:'The Thirteenth Hour',author:'NovelNest Originals',genre:'Mystery',tags:['Mystery','Fantasy'],status:'Completed',cover:'assets/hour.webp',updated:'2026-10-03',sample:true,license:{type:'Original demonstration content',note:'Created for the NovelNest website demonstration.'},synopsis:'The library clock strikes thirteen only when a book goes missing. On his first night at work, a new librarian discovers a locked room, an unfinished confession, and an hour nobody remembers.',chapters:[
 {title:'One chime too many',paragraphs:[
 'The clock struck thirteen at midnight. Elias counted twice, then looked up from the returns desk.',
 'His instructions were clear: lock the doors, put out the lamps, and never wind the brass clock. He had obeyed every word. Yet somewhere in the silent library, a door had opened.',
 'He followed the sound to the history shelves. Between two tall bookcases stood a narrow doorway he had not seen that afternoon. Warm light shone beneath it.',
 'Inside was a desk, a chair, and an open book. The first page bore his predecessor’s name. The next page held a single line: I did not steal the atlas.',
 'Elias remembered the newspaper story. A valuable atlas had disappeared last winter. The old librarian had left town soon afterward.',
 'A fresh line appeared beneath the first. You have one hour to find the missing page.',
 'Behind him, the library clock began to tick backward.' ]},
 {title:'The mark in the dust',paragraphs:[
 'Elias started with the atlas shelf. Dust covered the empty space, except for a thin crescent near the back. Someone had recently moved the book beside it.',
 'He pulled out a guide to local birds. A folded receipt slipped to the floor. It listed a repair to the library’s roof on the same day the atlas had vanished.',
 'Upstairs, rainwater had stained a ceiling beam. Below it stood a cabinet full of damaged books. Elias found the atlas there, its swollen cover wrapped in cloth. It had never left the building.',
 'But a page was missing from its middle. The torn edge was too neat to be an accident.',
 'He searched the repairs ledger. The previous librarian had paid for the damaged roof himself. Beside the payment, a short note had been crossed out: Page removed to protect the donor’s letter.',
 'A letter was hidden in the atlas, and someone wanted it forgotten. Elias looked toward the clock. Its hands had reached half past twelve. He had thirty minutes left.' ]},
 {title:'What the library kept',paragraphs:[
 'The donor’s portrait hung above the staircase. Elias lifted its frame and found a yellowed page tucked behind the backing.',
 'It was a map on one side, a letter on the other. The donor had left the building to the town, with a condition: its doors must remain open to everyone. The current trustees had been planning to sell it.',
 'The old librarian had hidden the letter when they ordered him to destroy it. Then he had vanished, hoping someone would finish what he could not.',
 'Elias carried the page into the secret room. The open book filled with words. They were not a confession after all. They were a witness statement.',
 'When the clock struck midnight again, the doorway disappeared. Elias stood between the shelves, holding the atlas, the letter, and a perfectly ordinary hour in his memory.',
 'At dawn, he made copies for the town council and the newspaper. Then he opened the front doors.',
 'The brass clock ticked quietly above the desk. That night, it struck only twelve.' ]}]}];
